Falsifies `measured_boot::verification::only_a_labeled_sha256_parses`.

The defect: the label is compared as a prefix of `sha256` rather than for equality, the shape a
reader that wanted to be lenient about a truncated label would write. `sha256:` still parses, and
every digest this tree writes still reads back.

Expected to fail: the harness's `assert_eq!(len, DIGEST_TEXT_LEN)`. A text with an empty or shorter
label (`:` and 64 hex, or `sha:` and 64 hex) now parses as a SHA-256, which is the label the ruling
exists to make mandatory being treated as optional.

diff --git a/crates/measured_boot/src/lib.rs b/crates/measured_boot/src/lib.rs
--- a/crates/measured_boot/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/measured_boot/src/lib.rs
@@ -391,7 +391,7 @@ fn parse_digest_bytes(text: &[u8]) -> Result<Digest, DigestTextError> {
     let Some(colon) = text.iter().position(|&b| b == b':') else {
         return Err(DigestTextError::Unlabeled);
     };
-    if text[..colon] != *SHA256_LABEL.as_bytes() {
+    if !SHA256_LABEL.as_bytes().starts_with(&text[..colon]) {
         return Err(DigestTextError::UnknownLabel);
     }
     parse_hex(&text[colon + 1..]).ok_or(DigestTextError::NotHex)
